>>
>> Have you ever thought about randomizing the effect resp. the
>> appearance of the dragon scale mails (and the dragons breath attacks)?
>> This would immediately void the possibility to wish for a specific
>> dragon scale mail as long as one hasn't seen the corresponding dragon
>> attack.
>> In my view randomness is a defining property of NetHack. Almost all
>> items are part of the ID game.
>> Why not dragon scales, too?
>
> Because of the goofy color associations, which are unfortunately an
> all-too-common trope and thus an aid to newer players encountering
> dragons for the first time.  :/

Is this really the case? I thought this color code was mainly a D&D
thing and besides something obvious like red dragon -> fire and white
dragon -> cold isn't really used anywhere else.

I have never been a D&D player so I don't know if knowledge of it
would have helped me the first time I met dragons in NetHack.

But if I look at the Wikipedia pages for dragons in D&D[1][2], I don't
really see, how it could have helped. 

The breath attacks fit more or less, but the breath attack is usually
the first thing you see of a dragon in NetHack. :)

AFAIK there are no dragon scale mails in D&D. So a D&D player wouldn't
know that they confer resistances?


BTW, I associate Green dragons with water because of a animation series
I saw as a child[3]. One cannot anticipate everybodys cultural
background. :)

